dia/awami-league"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awami-league.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awami League</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awami League is a political party of Bangladesh, founded in Dhaka in 1949 as a rival to the Muslim League in East Pakistan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awariku"><span class="enc-name">Awariku</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awariku, called Urikki in Assyrian texts, was a king of Que (Hiyawa) on the Cilician plain in the 8th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awash-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awash-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awash River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awash River is a river in Ethiopia that flows from the central highlands towards the Afar lowlands.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awdaghast"><span class="enc-name">Awdaghast</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awdaghast was a trading town in the southern Sahara, in present-day Mauritania, that served as a terminus of trans-Saharan caravan routes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-awhad-ayyub"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-awhad-ayyub.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Awhad Ayyub</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Awhad Ayyub was an Ayyubid prince, son of al-Adil, who governed Mayyafariqin in Upper Mesopotamia for his father from about 1200.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awilum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awilum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awīlum</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">In the Code of Hammurabi, the Babylonian legal text composed about 1753 BCE, the awīlum was one of three main kinds of person, beside the muškēnum and the slave.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/awjila"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/awjila.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Awjila</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Awjila is an oasis town in the Libyan Desert of eastern Libya, some 250 km inland from the Gulf of Sirte.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-balad"><span class="enc-name">Awlad al-balad</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The awlad al-balad, meaning 'sons of the country', were the noble ruling elite of the Wadai Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awlad-al-nas"><span class="enc-name">Awlad al-nas</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Awlad al-nas, &quot;sons of respectable people&quot;, was the name for the sons of mamluks in the Mamluk Sultanate, which ruled Egypt and Syria from 1250 to 1517.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awoamefia"><span class="enc-name">Awoamefia</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Awoamefia is the title of the paramount ruler of the Anlo Ewe of south-eastern Ghana, who resides at Anloga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awole"><span class="enc-name">Awole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Awole was an Alaafin of the Oyo Empire in the late eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/awori"><span class="enc-name">Awori</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Awori are a Yoruba subgroup of the coastal lagoons